    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "趨也。从夭、止。夭止者，屈也。凡走之屬皆从走。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Pictogram (象形): a running man. An additional 止 (“foot”) was added later. The variant form 𧺆 preserves the form in the Small Seal Script.\nIn the modern form, the top component (夭) is simplified to the unrelated 土 and the bottom part is stylized as 龰. See also the top component of 去 and 赤.\nAs with 圭, there are two separate vertical strokes, not a single one.\nSchuessler (2007) considers this to be part of a large Sino-Tibetan word family, including 搜 (OC *sru, “to move”), 速 (OC *sloːɡ, “quick”), 夙 (OC *suɡ, “morning”), 聳 (OC *sloŋʔ, “to excite”), 蔌 (OC *sloːɡ, “vegetables”), which derive from Proto-Sino-Tibetan *m-sow (“to arise; to awake”)."
    }
  ],
  "shuowen": "趨也。从夭、止。夭止者，屈也。凡走之屬皆从走。",
  "shuowenExplanation": "hasten. Ideographic compound of 夭、止 \"young\" and 走 \"walk\". All characters in the 走 \"walk\" category use this as radical.",
